I submit the attached technical statement as an independent software and
formal-methods researcher; it asks the Commission to approve no product. It
is argued from a staged claim protocol I built and operate as research,
running in local test banks and in an author-operated deployment on Solana's
devnet test cluster with valueless tokens --- no mainnet venue, accepted
funds, customers, or live orders. It analyzes programmable transactions whose character changes as
code is published, signed, funded, matched, resolved, and settled; the
positions are mine, argued from the statutory and regulatory text and one
worked example.

= Eight positions

1. Analysis should attach to milestones --- publication, funding, close,
   resolution, settlement --- not to labels; this settles which facts each
   legal question reads.
2. Publication of market software, without more, should not itself be a
   regulated activity --- it creates no contract, transaction, or exposure;
   operator analysis begins at funding and interaction, not authorship.
3. Instrument creation, collateral lock, gross claim issuance, and net
   contingent exposure are distinct milestones. In the worked example,
   funding issues gross claims and fixes maximum payout liability, while a
   complete set remains net-flat until an unbalancing transfer.
4. Prefunding can remove novation, credit extension, and loss mutualization,
   but does not itself decide DCO status: analysis should identify any
   multilateral settlement or netting function, custody, settlement control,
   and failure handling.
5. The fields a DCM must read in real time are those its surveillance and
   reconstruction obligations consume; others may remain encrypted where
   exact linked records stay timely recoverable by the regulated function
   and the Commission --- the real-time-visibility floor.
6. Governed threshold disclosure can satisfy those objectives without
   routine plaintext visibility to one commercial operator --- the
   disclosure target --- while whether an architecture with no general
   opening path is precluded remains a research question.
7. Independently verifiable proofs should count as evidence of exactly
   what their statements encode, with underlying records preserved and a
   structured predeployment pathway with the relevant operating division;
   this settles the evidentiary role of proofs.
8. Removing settlement discretion relocates manipulation risk to the
   reference market rather than removing it; terms should state the full
   reference specification so surveillance can evaluate an
   assumption-indexed manipulation-cost envelope and know what to watch and
   when.

*Scope.* Current Regulation 40.11 and a pending June 2026 proposal address event
contracts involving enumerated activities and public-interest
review.#note_ref(1) The attached examples concern objectively verifiable
crypto-native ledger states, program events, prices, ranges, and path
statistics --- not politics, sports, gaming, or subjective social events. This scope is not a claim that any example falls outside the
CEA or other law.

The attachment uses Clear, Shielded, and Dark only as exact information-flow
terms: regulator-observable Shielded is the reference; Dark is a research
boundary. Its technical claims are stated with their evidence and their
boundaries in place --- resolution authenticates one canonical sealed source
receipt while the default build fails closed with no registered source
provider, and the measured compute stops are reported as numbers --- and
nothing in it asserts a complete venue, production adapter, whole-system
proof, or legal conclusion.

